\section{Ordering}{

By default a package's tutorials are listed in the order of their
directory names, so authors usually number them (\code{01-intro},
\code{02-data}, ...). A tutorial can instead set its position in its YAML
header, without renaming its directory (a directory name is the
tutorial's id, so renaming one breaks links and render caches):

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode yaml">}}\preformatted{learnr2:
  ordering: 3
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{available_tutorials()} reports it in the \code{ordering} column. Tools that
list tutorials, such as the "R Tutorials" VS Code extension, sort a
package's tutorials by \code{ordering} (lowest first), with tutorials that
don't set it after those that do, in directory-name order. A value that
is not a single number is ignored (reported as \code{NA}); \code{\link[=check_tutorial]{check_tutorial()}}
flags it.
}

\section{Classic learnr tutorials}{

A \code{"quarto"} tutorial's exercises run in the reader's browser via WebR, so
it needs no R packages installed locally beyond learnr2 itself and its
\code{package_dependencies} is \code{character(0)}. An \code{"rmarkdown"} tutorial is a
classic 'learnr' tutorial (an \code{.Rmd} with \code{runtime: shiny_prerendered}),
which runs as a Shiny app in the local R session. Its
\code{package_dependencies} are whatever 'learnr' finds by scanning the
tutorial's directory (\code{learnr::available_tutorials()}), which always
includes 'learnr' itself. If 'learnr' is not installed there is nothing
to ask, and such a tutorial could not run anyway, so the entry is \code{NA}.
}

\section{Packages loaded with pkgload}{

\code{system.file()} resolves against the \emph{installed} copy of a package, so a
content package under development used to be invisible here (or, worse,
silently read from an old install) when its own tests ran under
\code{devtools::test()}: 'pkgload' redirects \code{system.file()} only for code
inside the package being developed, not for learnr2's calls. This
function and \code{\link[=run_tutorial]{run_tutorial()}} therefore check whether \code{package} is a
namespace loaded by \code{pkgload::load_all()} and, if so, read its tutorials from the
source tree's \verb{inst/tutorials/} directly. Nothing changes for installed
packages, and 'pkgload' itself is not required.
